    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **255 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `riskFlag` derived field on the owner response so it's `true` when **any** of the three conditions hold, and `false` otherwise.\n\n**What I changed:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a `riskFlag` boolean (`readOnly`) property to the `OwnerDto` schema, documenting the three-part rule. This regenerates `OwnerDto` with `getRiskFlag()/setRiskFlag()`.\n\n2. **`DisposableEmailDomains.java`** (new) \u2014 Centralizes disposable-domain knowledge. The exact blocklist (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`) is still rejected at create time; this class adds the softer **disposable-adjacent** notion: a domain whose *second-level label* matches a known disposable service (catching a different TLD like `mailinator.net` and subdomains like `inbox.mailinator.com`), without flagging unrelated domains.\n\n3. **`RejectDisposableEmailDomain.java`** \u2014 Refactored to reuse the shared blocklist via `DisposableEmailDomains.isBlocked(...)`, so the reject rule and the risk assessment stay consistent.\n\n4. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added the `riskFlag` mapping and a `riskFlag(Owner)` helper that ORs the three signals:\n   - **possible duplicate** \u2192 `possibleDuplicateOf != null` (same source as the existing `possibleDuplicate` field),\n   - **disposable-adjacent email** \u2192 `DisposableEmailDomains.isDisposableAdjacent(email)`,\n   - **city over soft capacity** \u2192 `capacityWarning` (the 40+ band, already computed on both create and read by `AssignOwnerCapacityWarning`).\n\nComputing `riskFlag` in the mapper \u2014 rather than as a new pipeline step \u2014 reuses the flags already present on the `Owner` at response time, so no POST/GET YAML wiring changes were needed. The new acceptance test (`coreRiskFlagFalseNormally`) and all 255 existing tests pass.\n\nOne design note worth flagging: I interpreted \"over its soft capacity\" as the existing `capacityWarning` band (40\u201349 others). If your spec intends \"soft capacity\" as a distinct numeric threshold (e.g. strictly greater than 40, or including cities already at the hard cap of 50 in legacy/seed data), let me know and I'll adjust the threshold or move the count into a dedicated function step with repository access.",
